## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of low-level synchronization primitives, cache-line considerations, and safe reuse of barriers in multi-threaded code.

## Key Concepts

- Sense-reversing barrier pattern
- Cache-line padding to avoid false sharing
- Atomic operations and memory_order semantics

## Code Example

```c
// barrier_init
void barrier_init(barrier_t *b, int n) { b->n = n; b->count = 0; b->sense = false; }

// barrier_wait
void barrier_wait(barrier_t *b, bool *local) {
    bool local_sense = !*local;
    if (atomic_fetch_add(&b->count, 1) == b->n - 1) {
        atomic_store(&b->count, 0);
        atomic_store(&b->sense, local_sense);
    } else {
        while (atomic_load(&b->sense) != local_sense) pause();
    }
    *local = local_sense;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt to dynamic N?
- How to measure wake-up latency under contention?

## Why This Is Asked

The task probes practical memory allocator design under NUMA, highlighting locality, contention, and fragmentation trade-offs with lock-free data structures.

## Key Concepts

- NUMA locality
- Per-thread caches
- Lock-free freelists and memory reclamation
- Fragmentation and refill strategies
- Benchmark design

## Code Example

```c
// High-level sketch of data structures
typedef struct Block { struct Block *next; } Block;
typedef struct { _Atomic(Block*) central; Block *local; int node; } Allocator;

void* malloc64(Allocator *A){
  Block *b = A->local; if(b){ A->local = b->next; return (void*)b; }
  Block *c = atomic_load_explicit(&A->central, memory_order_acquire);
  if(c){ atomic_store_explicit(&A->central, c->next, memory_order_release); return (void*)c; }
  // refill from OS or malloc from system
  return system_alloc(A);
}
void free64(Allocator *A, Block *b){
  b->next = A->local; A->local = b;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle thread migration across NUMA nodes? 
- What metrics identify improvement (latency, bandwidth, cache misses)?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests basic concurrent data structure design and practical use of atomics and memory ordering in a beginner-friendly but realistic setting.

## Key Concepts
- MPSC synchronization using a shared tail and a local consumer head
- Atomic fetch_add for slot reservation
- Memory ordering: release on enqueue, acquire on dequeue
- Correct wrap-around handling in a fixed-size buffer

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-C code (C11 atomics style)
#define N (1<<12)
typedef struct { uint64_t data; uint64_t seq; } slot_t;
slot_t buf[N];
atomic_uintptr_t tail = 0; // producer index
size_t head = 0; // consumer index

bool enqueue(uint64_t v){
  size_t t = atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&tail, 1, memory_order_relaxed);
  if (t >= N) return false;
  buf[t].data = v;
  atomic_thread_fence(memory_order_release);
  buf[t].seq = t; // simple wrap-safe signal
  return true;
}

bool dequeue(uint64_t* out){
  if (head >= atomic_load_explicit(&tail, memory_order_acquire)) return false;
  *out = buf[head].data;
  head++;
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this to detect and recover from corrupted data?
- How would you benchmark throughput under 1000 producers vs 1 consumer?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of crash-consistent data structures, persistent memory programming, and low-latency recovery mechanisms in real-world systems, connecting hardware flush instructions to correct ordering guarantees.

## Key Concepts
- Non-volatile memory semantics and persistence instructions (clwb, sfence)
- Write-ahead logging protocols and commit/rollback mechanisms for crash safety
- Epoch-based reclamation and idempotent recovery procedures
- Log replay and in-memory index reconstruction after power loss
- NUMA-aware memory layout and efficient log space management

## Code Example
```c
// Minimal sketch of log append and commit protocol
typedef struct {
    uint64_t key;
    uint64_t val;
    uint64_t epoch;
    uint8_t committed; // 0=pending, 1=committed
} log_entry_t;

// Put operation with crash consistency
int kv_put(uint64_t key, uint64_t val) {
    log_entry_t *entry = alloc_log_entry();
    entry->key = key;
    entry->val = val;
    entry->epoch = get_current_epoch();
    entry->committed = 0; // pending

    _mm_clwb(entry); // flush to NVRAM
    _mm_sfence();    // ensure ordering

    entry->committed = 1; // mark committed
    _mm_clwb(&entry->committed);
    _mm_sfence();

    update_index(key, val); // update in-memory index
    return 0;
}
```

